#---
# purpose: "a loop that tail-calls its own function yields at its quantum and resumes with its current values, so a second process runs while the loop is still going"
# kind: run
#---
#
# `Loop.sum/2` is a two-clause function that calls itself in tail position two
# million times, far past one scheduling quantum. The loop's process is spawned
# first and the short process second, so a loop that never yielded would finish
# before the short process ran and `:short` would arrive second. Yielding lets
# the short process send first; the loop then resumes from the values it held
# at each yield and still reaches the exact sum. The interpreter spends no
# reductions and never yields, so the loop finishes first there.
defmodule Loop do
  def sum(0, acc), do: acc
  def sum(n, acc), do: sum(n - 1, acc + n)
end

def main() do
  parent = self()
  spawn(fn () -> send(parent, {:sum, Loop.sum(2000000, 0)}) end)
  spawn(fn () -> send(parent, :short) end)
  first = receive do
    msg -> msg
  end
  second = receive do
    msg -> msg
  end
  dbg(first)
  dbg(second)
end
